--- body ---
## Context

During a bulk file sync, every attachment Zotero has not yet downloaded reads
as `missing-source`: the `missingSource()` closure in
`inspect()` (`bootstrap.js:3269-3283` at v0.4.22 — cited by symbol because
0796 moved every line in this region) sets that status when
`getFilePathAsync()` returns nothing or `IOUtils.stat()` throws `NotFoundError`,
which is the correct answer about a file that is genuinely not on disk yet.
The census is
therefore not wrong — it is a true account of a moment that the sync makes
obsolete within minutes.

Two costs, and the second is the one that matters:

1. The work is wasted. Classifying 13 700 attachments that are about to change
   state, then re-classifying them, is exactly the footprint SPEC.md's "quiet
   supervisor" paragraph exists to bound (ticket 0701).
2. **The display misleads.** A reader sees a full categorical breakdown, stated
   with the same confidence as in steady state, beside an age of three minutes.
   Nothing in the panel says the classification was taken against a library
   that was 9 % present. Tonight this misled the author and this session at
   once, and only measuring the filesystem settled it.

## What already exists, and must be reused

- `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` (`bootstrap.js:744` at v0.4.22; it was 718 before 0796
  moved this file) already held five refusal phases,
  and ticket **0745** already made a blocked phase back off to the 10-minute
  idle cadence instead of the 30-second active one. A sixth phase inherits
  both the backoff and the phase label. Nothing new is needed for the
  mechanism — only for the detection.
- Ticket **0759**'s ruling governs the display half: *"RULING: live. Accept the
  truth and disclose it, rather than hiding or debouncing it."* So a pause must
  not merely freeze the numbers. The phase label is what discloses the reason,
  and that is the half of this change the ruling makes mandatory rather than
  optional.

## The unknown, and why it is measured before anything is designed

The sitter has **zero** references to `Zotero.Sync`, `syncInProgress` or
`Sync.Runner` in `bootstrap.js` and `scheduler.js`. Whether Zotero 10 exposes a
readable sync state to a bootstrap extension is unestablished here.

This matters more than the usual missing-API question because the five existing
phases all read the **machine** (`/proc/meminfo`, disk, CPU) while this one
would read the **host application** — the contract class where this codebase
has already been burned once: the `LINK_MODE_LINKED_FILE` guard in `inspect()`
(`bootstrap.js:3250-3262` at v0.4.22) carries a guard written
against an assumed host contract that made every attachment read as a linked
file. A phase that cannot fire, or that fires always, is worse than no phase.

**This is exactly why v0.4.22 ships the phase and NOT a measurement.** The
`syncing()` predicate is one named function whose body is the whole of the
unmeasured contract; the policy it feeds is tested through `host.blocked`, a
contract this repository owns. Nothing was added to
`tests/sdt_sitter_zotero_mock.mjs`, so the harness still publishes no
`Zotero.Sync` shape and cannot vouch for one.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — `SDT_SYNC_PHASE` / `SDT_SYNC_PHASE_LABEL`
  and `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` (744), `nextSweepDelayMS` (773), `syncing()` and
  `blocked()` (3412+), `SDT_PHASE_LABELS` (1050), `SDT_TEXT` (340). Line numbers
  are v0.4.22 and shifted by ~66 when 0796 landed: re-derive by symbol.
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` — `record()`, `publish()`; sets `phase`
  from `host.blocked()`
- `tests/sdt_sitter_zotero_mock.mjs` — the mock host; see the warning below
- `tests/test_sdt_sitter.py`, `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs`

## Actions

1. **Measure the host contract first.** In the author's Zotero console, while a
   sync is running, enumerate what is readable rather than guessing a name:

   ```js
   (() => {
     const R = Zotero.Sync?.Runner, S = Zotero.Sync?.Storage;
     return JSON.stringify({
       runner: !!R,
       syncInProgress: R?.syncInProgress ?? null,
       runnerKeys: R ? Object.keys(R).filter(k => !k.startsWith('_')) : null,
       storageKeys: S ? Object.keys(S) : null,
       localKeys: S?.Local ? Object.keys(S.Local).filter(k => !k.startsWith('_')) : null,
     }, null, 2);
   })()
   ```

   Record the output in this ticket. Re-run it with sync idle: a key that reads
   the same in both states does not discriminate and must not be used.

   **Neither arm is available on demand, and the active one is the scarce half.**
   The reading needs a live Zotero window with the Run JavaScript pane — no
   headless session, no RDP client against an instance that was not launched with
   `--start-debugger-server`, can take it, and relaunching for the flag destroys
   whatever state was being observed. The idle arm only costs a session. The
   active arm additionally needs a real bulk sync in progress, which is not
   something to provoke: it is hours of transfer and, until 0796 is fixed, it
   starves the sitter for the duration. So this measurement waits for the next
   sync that happens anyway. Plan the ticket around catching one, not around
   scheduling one.
2. Decide the fallback when the API is absent or unreadable. The default this
   ticket proposes: behave exactly as today. A sitter that pauses because it
   cannot tell is a sitter that never runs.
3. Add the phase, its label, and its entry in `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES`.
   **Done, v0.4.22.** `SDT_SYNC_PHASE` / `SDT_SYNC_PHASE_LABEL`, the
   `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` entry, the `SDT_TEXT` wording, and a `syncing()`
   predicate beside `workerBusy()` in `blocked()`. The predicate body is the
   only unmeasured line and is uncovered by design — see the experiment below.
4. Ensure a sync ending wakes the sitter rather than leaving it parked for the
   full 10-minute idle interval.
   **Deliberately NOT done, and this is a second unmeasured platform contract,
   which this action did not flag as one.** A wake-on-completion needs Zotero's
   Notifier to carry a sync lifecycle signal. Nothing in the plugin observes one
   today, and whether one exists is exactly as unmeasured as
   `syncInProgress` — registering an observer for a topic nobody has seen fire
   would be the same guess this ticket refuses to make about the key. Shipped
   instead: the flat 10-minute idle poll every other blocked phase already pays,
   which ends the pause on its own and is already correct, only slower. The
   question is folded into the same live sitting as the first reading, so one
   session settles both.

## The experiment, for the author, in one sitting

Open Zotero's Tools → Developer → Run JavaScript panel and paste Action 1's
snippet **twice** — once while a bulk file sync is actually transferring, once
with sync idle — pasting the output of each run into this ticket labelled
`ACTIVE` and `IDLE`; then, in the same sitting and in the same panel, paste
`Object.keys(Zotero.Notifier._observers || {})` together with
`JSON.stringify(Zotero.Notifier.Types ?? Object.keys(Zotero.Notifier))` and
record that too, because it answers the second question (does a sync lifecycle
signal exist that a bootstrap extension could subscribe to, so a finished sync
wakes the sitter instead of it waiting out ten minutes). **The decision rule is
that a field is adopted only if it DIFFERS between the two arms**: a key reading
`true` in both, `false` in both, or absent in both is disqualified outright and
is not a result to be worked around or averaged — it means the key cannot see a
sync, and the ticket then either names a different field from the same dump or
closes with the measured reason no field discriminates. Whichever field wins,
wiring it is a one-line edit to `syncing()` in `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js`
and nothing else; if the Notifier dump shows a sync topic, action 4 becomes a
second small edit, and if it shows none, action 4 closes as measured-impossible
rather than staying open on hope.

## What this ticket exposes for ticket 0797

0797's state table puts `sync in progress` on the **checked, non-interactive**
side, beside the boot wait, while the default `a blocked phase` row is
**unchecked and interactive**. So `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES.includes(phase)` is NOT
sufficient logic for 0797: it must special-case this phase by name. Two names
are exported from `bootstrap.js` for it to read, and they are the only two it
needs:

- `SDT_SYNC_PHASE` — the phase string, `'sync-in-progress'`. It is what
  `state.phase` reads while the pause holds, and `blocked()` returns the literal
  so the scheduler suite's phase enumeration still sees it; a test asserts the
  literal and the constant are the same string.
- `SDT_SYNC_PHASE_LABEL` — the message id, `'phase-sync-in-progress'`, whose
  `SDT_TEXT` wording is **"Waiting for sync to finish"**, matching 0797's table
  row verbatim so 0797 reuses this string instead of authoring a second one.
  A mutant (M80) reddens on a reword, so the two cannot drift apart silently.

## Test

Red step, in the scheduler suite: a mock host reporting a sync in progress must
leave `phase` at the new value and `nextSweepDelayMS` at the idle cadence.
Reverting the `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` entry must reproduce the active cadence —
the same red-first shape ticket 0745 used for the five existing phases.

**The mock must not invent the platform.** The `LINK_MODE_LINKED_FILE` guard in
`inspect()` (`bootstrap.js:3250-3262` at v0.4.22) records
what happens when the harness publishes a host API the real Zotero shapes
differently: every fixture read as a linked file and the defect was invisible
until a real host disagreed. Shape the mock from the step-1 output, not from
what the API ought to look like.

**What v0.4.22 actually shipped, and what it deliberately left uncovered.** The
red step is `a sync in progress refuses admission, backs off to the idle
cadence, and says why` in `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs`, driving
`host.blocked` — the same honest fake the five existing phases use. It asserts
both sides of the membership boundary: the idle cadence for the phase on
`SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES`, and the active 30 s cadence for a phase off it, so the
first assertion cannot pass vacuously. Three mutants in
`verification/probes/sdt_sitter_bootstrap_mutants.py` run it against a
deliberately broken implementation — M78 drops the `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` entry
(the ticket's own "reverting the entry reproduces the active cadence"), M79
drops the label, M80 rewords it — and all three are caught. **No test asserts
the `syncing()` body**, and none may until the reading exists: a mutant of that
line would survive by construction, which is why the probe carries a written
note in place of one rather than a fourth mutant.

## Decision for the author

**On a fresh install the first run is a large sync, so this pauses the sitter
for precisely the session where a new user is watching it.** Indexing files
that are still arriving is wasted work — they are re-hashed once settled — but
the cost is that first value arrives later, and the panel says "waiting" during
the one session that decides whether the plugin looks alive.

Accepting that is the proposal. The alternative is to pause the **census** and
let admission keep indexing whatever is already on disk, which keeps the
counter moving at the price of a second, narrower rule. Not chosen here.

## Invariants

- The five existing phases and 0745's backoff keep their current behaviour.
- 0759 stands: totals stay live and disclosed, never debounced or hidden.
- No new library write, and no new periodic cost while idle.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Step 1's console output recorded in this ticket, with the sync-idle
      control, and a named key that discriminates between the two states
      — **OPEN, and it is what keeps this ticket open.** Needs the author's live
      Zotero window; see "The experiment, for the author, in one sitting" above.
      Until it exists, `syncing()` reads a candidate path and the phase can
      never fire, which is the honest state and not a silent one.
- [x] The phase ships with a label, or the ticket closes with the measured
      reason it cannot — v0.4.22: `phase-sync-in-progress`, "Waiting for sync to
      finish", mutants M79 and M80
- [x] Red-first test in the scheduler suite, reverting the entry reproduces the
      active cadence — v0.4.22: the scheduler test named above, plus mutant M78,
      which IS the reverted entry and is caught
- [ ] Sync completion wakes the sitter without waiting out the idle interval
      — **OPEN and reframed.** This rests on a second unmeasured platform
      contract (does Zotero's Notifier carry a sync lifecycle signal?), folded
      into the same experiment. v0.4.22 ships the flat 10-minute idle poll,
      which ends the pause on its own.
- [x] Three reads of `state.draining` on a live syncing instance, recorded in
      `verification/incidents/0795-2026-09-15-drain-during-sync.md`: falling
      confirms the race reading, holding or rising splits a new ticket off
      — taken 2026-09-15: 6495 / 6543 / 6573 against 761 at `drain-start`. They
      ROSE, so the race reading was refuted and ticket 0796 was split off; it
      merged 2026-09-15 and bounded the drain, which is also what makes this
      ticket's check reachable at all (before it, `blocked()` was not called
      once in eight minutes during the captured incident).
- [ ] `make check` passes — green for v0.4.22; re-run when the reading lands
